insert into public.articles (id, drive_file_id, drive_modified_at, title, slug, meta_description, content_type_id, service_area_id, target_keyword, secondary_keywords, tags, author_name, publish_date, body_markdown, status, owner_id, submitted_at, approved_by, approved_at, published_at, published_url, created_at, updated_at) values ('83eecaea-5599-4ee1-ac97-07957aeec61c', 'seed_1waatF3XQU7e5fAN6wrW3PmCesc7', '2026-06-22T09:45:32.000Z', 'EPC Contracts: What Renewables Owners Should Know', 'epc-contracts-what-renewables-owners-should-know-2', 'EPC contracts: how EPC contract management cuts cost and schedule risk on Renewables projects in northern Alberta.', (select id from public.content_types where slug = 'whitepaper'), (select id from public.service_areas where slug = 'c2b-c2c-contracts'), 'EPC contracts', array['Pre construction'], array['c2b-c2c-contracts', 'renewables', 'northern alberta'], 'EPCMst Team', '2026-08-26', 'When Renewables owners in northern Alberta search for "EPC contracts", they usually want one thing: a project that stays on budget and on schedule. Regulators and Indigenous partners expect early, honest engagement, and projects go smoother when they get it. That means naming one accountable person and a clear decision date. Measuring progress weekly, with the same yardstick, keeps surprises small. We recommend a short review at thirty, sixty and ninety percent design for every tailings facility.
